One important correction

Your configure_mlflow() does this:

mlflow.set_registry_uri(MLFLOW_REGISTRY_URI)

when Unity Catalog is disabled.

For this test, that means your registry URI is controlled by the constant in config.py, not necessarily the temporary MLflow server.

If MLFLOW_REGISTRY_URI is something like:

MLFLOW_REGISTRY_URI = "databricks"

then your integration test could accidentally try to use Databricks.

For a completely local integration test, I'd want:

Tracking URI  → temporary MLflow server
Registry URI  → same temporary MLflow server
Backend       → temporary SQLite
Artifacts     → temporary artifact directory

So your test should ideally be able to configure both tracking and registry to the temporary server.

I would make one small production-code improvement

Change configure_mlflow() to optionally accept the tracking/registry URI:

def configure_mlflow(
    use_unity_catalog: bool,
    uc_catalog: str | None,
    uc_schema: str | None,
    base_experiment_name: str,
    product_name: str,
    experiment_root_directory: str | None,
    tracking_uri: str | None = None,
    registry_uri: str | None = None,
) -> dict[str, Any]:

Then:

if tracking_uri:
    mlflow.set_tracking_uri(tracking_uri)

if registry_uri:
    mlflow.set_registry_uri(registry_uri)

Your integration test can then do:

configure_mlflow(
    use_unity_catalog=False,
    uc_catalog=None,
    uc_schema=None,
    base_experiment_name="SummarizationIntegrationTest",
    product_name="TestProduct",
    experiment_root_directory="/",
    tracking_uri=mlflow_server,
    registry_uri=mlflow_server,
)

That is much cleaner for your MLflow integration testing, because the test becomes completely isolated from your real Databricks/MLflow configuration.

One other thing: your current log_transformer_model() doesn't actually use the signature you're calculating. If schema enforcement is one of the things you specifically want this integration test to verify, we should modify log_transformer_model() to accept and pass the signature to mlflow.transformers.log_model().
